{
  "reasoning": "Turn 0 executed multiple wc calls sequentially (mode l, w, c) and appended narrative summary instead of yielding JSON-only output. Turn 1 chained cd→ls→wc operations in one turn rather than executing atomically per operation. The file system state diverged because narrative text was included instead of pure tool tuples, and exploratory commands (ls) were bundled with state-reading operations (wc).",
  "error_identification": "Turn 0: Missing atomic separation—executed wc three times in sequence with narrative appended (delta23, delta39 violation). Turn 1: Chained cd+ls+wc in single turn instead of one atomic operation per turn (delta9, delta19 violation). Model included explanatory text after tool output rather than JSON-only format.",
  "root_cause_analysis": "Model violated delta23/delta39 (no narrative after tool output) and delta9/delta19 (one atomic operation per turn, no chaining exploratory with read operations). The state mismatch occurred because the evaluation framework tracks execution tuples strictly; appending narrative and chaining operations altered the expected execution sequence and state accumulation.",
  "correct_approach": "Turn 0: Execute mkdir, find, cp, find, grep, wc (single mode) atomically in sequence—yield JSON tool tuples only, no narrative. Turn 1: Execute cd atomically; yield. Next turn (if needed): Execute ls atomically; yield. Next turn: Execute wc on summary.doc; yield. Next turn: Execute mean calculation; yield. Return only JSON arrays of tool calls per turn.",
  "key_insight": "Multi-turn file system evaluation requires strict separation of atomic operations and JSON-only output. Narrative summaries and chained exploratory commands (ls, cd) with read operations (wc) break state accumulation. Each turn must contain one logical atomic operation; defer all analysis to user-initiated turns."
}

